WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.264
How are you doing during quarantine?

00:00:02.264 --> 00:00:05.440
How are you as a human
being, not an alien,

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.433
a human being handling quarantine?

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:13.140
Well, you know, one aspect
of it has been very lovely,

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.840
I've been alone with my wife

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:18.616
and it's been four months now

00:00:18.616 --> 00:00:20.830
and it's almost as if we've been making up

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:25.480
for all those times in
our like 40 year marriage,

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.370
when her business or
mine have separated us,

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.710
she's a professor, I'm an actor,

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:35.760
I've spent like 30% of
that time on location,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.050
20% on a film set in LA.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:40.740
This was like making up for all that,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.560
we just, it turns out we realized

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.010
we were even better company for each other

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.570
than we ever dreamed, it was lovely.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:52.530
Beyond that, I actually
had a creative project,

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:57.530
that kept me going up until
about three or four weeks ago

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.630
and that was I wrote an entire book.

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.570
I had a book contract to do a followup

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.810
of my political humor book, of "Dumpty,"

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.830
which came out last October,
that was a big success.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.900
My publisher begged me to do another one

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.500
in time for the election,
I said, no, no, no, no,

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.416
I finally said yes and
then I thought, oh, my God,

00:01:18.416 --> 00:01:20.100
how am I ever gonna do this?

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.830
I'm working on a TV series.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:26.170
Well, along comes a global pandemic,

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:29.870
the series is suspended,
I have nothing to do,

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:33.810
but eat, sleep and cook.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.550
Okay, I'll be able to do this

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:39.230
and I wrote the entire book of 32 poems,

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:42.420
110 pages, did all the illustrations

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.470
and it'll be out on September 29th

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.410
in plenty of time for the election.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.489
When the first book came out,

00:01:47.489 --> 00:01:50.800
you appeared on the show and
you did a reading from it

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.630
and the book was a big success

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:55.510
and I was really impressed with the poetry

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:59.650
and with the illustrations,
really, really fantastic.

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:02.310
Well, and I got the Conan bump.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.330
You made me a New York
Times bestseller, Conan.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.620
Yes, I can do that too,
I can get you an Oscar

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.711
or New York Times, either one.

00:02:09.711 --> 00:02:12.731
That's why I'm here really.

00:02:12.731 --> 00:02:16.110
Yeah, you loathe to talk to
me, but you know you have to.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.520
I can read you from this new one too.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.360
Great, this is called a
"Trumpy Dumpty Wanted a Crown,

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:23.997
Verses for a Despotic Age"

00:02:23.997 --> 00:02:25.360
and I wanna tell you one thing,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:26.970
that should be very flattering,

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:31.040
I wanted to hold up the book
here in person to show it

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.160
and it was sent to us and
we're here at the theater

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:37.710
and it arrived at the
theater and someone stole it.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:38.543
Oh!

00:02:38.543 --> 00:02:41.840
That's a fine compliment
that your book was stolen,

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:42.673
Yeah, wow.

00:02:42.673 --> 00:02:44.380
from right here at the Largo Theater.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.040
But we will put it up and make sure

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.430
that we prominently display
it here on the screen.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:53.190
This is about Bill Barr
very much in our recent-

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:56.520
Bill Barr, Attorney General Bill Barr.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.180
Yeah, I'll show you
the illustration first,

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.291
because when you read the book,
this is what you would see.

00:03:02.291 --> 00:03:03.913
(Conan laughing)

00:03:03.913 --> 00:03:08.440
As you see, it's Bill Barr as a frog,

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.932
which how can you not
portray Bill Barr as a frog?

00:03:11.932 --> 00:03:13.560
[Conan] There he is.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.440
Sitting outside the Oval
Office standing guard,

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.700
it's called "Bill Barr, the Door."

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:24.700
AG Barr, that faithful sentry
guards the Oval Office entry,

00:03:24.958 --> 00:03:27.900
though he stays all legal action,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.530
he should check self satisfaction,

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.280
add a single scandal more

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.663
and he'll be frogmarched out the door.

00:03:35.793 --> 00:03:38.631
Frogmarched out the door, get it?

00:03:38.631 --> 00:03:40.910
I get it, that's classic.
That's the short one,

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:41.790
that's the short one,

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.080
some of them are two or
three or four pages long,

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.563
but I thought I'd give you a bon bon.

00:03:46.563 --> 00:03:50.250
That's terrific, now do
you think it's gonna be

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:53.367
highly read in the White House
when it comes out, you know?

00:03:53.367 --> 00:03:56.793
Do you think it's gonna be
a big hit with his crew?

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:00.520
Well, I certainly hope they'll read it,

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:04.212
because if they read it, maybe
I'll get a Tweet out of him,

00:04:04.212 --> 00:04:06.110
I'll sell thousands.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:10.180
I mean, you know, of course it's a book

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.573
to delight and amuse liberals,

00:04:15.123 --> 00:04:16.490
never Trumpers,

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:19.850
Democrats in anticipation of the election,

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:23.540
but I'm counting on a lot
of right wing fanatics

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.813
buying it too, they'll wanna
buy it in order to burn it.

00:04:27.846 --> 00:04:30.815
But they will have to buy it.

00:04:30.815 --> 00:04:33.870
A lot of us are angry,

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.800
but as you know, when you're
in the comedy business,

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.520
you're trying to walk a line

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.900
of not just being outraged,

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.270
you're trying to still
remember that your job

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:47.240
is to entertain people and
to get a smile out of them

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:48.073
and you took that

00:04:48.073 --> 00:04:51.180
and I think that's what
you've done with this.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:52.620
I have to say, Conan,

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.360
you are a much more
interesting interview subject

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.190
than I am in this matter.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.410
I mean, I am fascinated by the challenge,

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.030
that all of you are facing,

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:08.030
all of you late night wits and
satirists and commentators.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:12.760
How do you, I mean, how
do you feel about that?

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:15.780
You asked me how I felt
not having an audience

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:16.877
[Conan] Right.

00:05:16.877 --> 00:05:21.000
How do you feel when things
are so dire and so difficult

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.920
and so enraging, when
your job is to be funny?

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:27.828
Well, there are times where,

00:05:27.828 --> 00:05:30.710
there are times recently when
things have gotten so dark,

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:35.050
especially right after
George Floyd's killing,

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.787
that I just decided, you know,

00:05:37.787 --> 00:05:39.640
if someone knows how to try and make

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.830
this situation we're in right
now funny, God bless him,

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:44.840
I don't know how and
I don't even think it,

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.150
it didn't feel appropriate to me.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:47.570
So there are times when I think

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:48.900
you just have to be a human being

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:51.480
and when things get really
dark, you have to say,

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.102
this is not, I'm just
gonna talk about this,

00:05:54.102 --> 00:05:56.150
I'm gonna talk to people about it

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:59.330
and so you surrender,
you surrender I think

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.932
a little bit the comedic role

00:06:00.932 --> 00:06:02.083
and then the rest of the time,

00:06:02.083 --> 00:06:05.460
if you think it's at all
possible, you try and do it.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:07.032
But is it at all a relief,
Yes.

00:06:07.032 --> 00:06:09.652
that you don't have to
be funny on the subject?

00:06:09.652 --> 00:06:14.500
Sometimes it is, but
mostly if it's so serious,

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:18.460
that you can't even, that
you can't do what you,

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.050
I feel like I was put here to do,

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.380
which is to entertain
people and make them laugh,

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:27.153
if I can't do that, it's, you know,

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:32.030
I think it's at least
cathartic to talk to people

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:34.610
and say how do you feel about this?

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:37.230
And we can have a discussion,

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.760
even if it's not a funny discussion,

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:39.692
I think that's one of the rules.

00:06:39.692 --> 00:06:43.110
I mean, Johnny Carson,
I watched him growing up

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:47.106
and he would have guests on
and he wouldn't go for laughs

00:06:47.106 --> 00:06:50.200
and I think sometimes that's
completely appropriate.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:51.133
I think it's.

00:06:52.869 --> 00:06:57.773
Well, and you and a few
more of your cohort,

00:06:57.773 --> 00:07:00.200
I mean, there are those moments,

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:02.420
when things are too grave to be funny.

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:05.917
I mean, you think of Letterman
when he addressed the nation,

00:07:05.917 --> 00:07:09.187
when he first came on the air after 9/11.

00:07:09.187 --> 00:07:10.850
Yeah.

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:12.600
If you put up, had a list

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.611
of the 10 greatest Letterman moments,

00:07:14.611 --> 00:07:17.070
that would be right up near the top.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:20.700
Yep.
Moments when a funny person

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:24.030
just stops being funny
and talks from the heart

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:25.753
and is capable of doing that,
Right.

00:07:25.753 --> 00:07:28.450
with real integrity and authenticity,

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:31.770
that's got a tremendous amount of power

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:34.020
and I think it's essential for us all,

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.010
in a sense that's more comforting

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:39.000
than making people laugh in dark times.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.815
Yeah, I still think I always am relieved,

00:07:40.815 --> 00:07:45.320
when we get back to a situation
where I can be silly again,

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.253
silly is my, that's.

00:07:49.718 --> 00:07:52.850
I've never seen you be silly, Conan.

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:54.103
I know, I know.

00:07:55.949 --> 00:07:59.233
I know you as gravely serious.

00:08:01.426 --> 00:08:02.900
You're right, occasionally,

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:05.633
I'll find evidence that
I was occasionally silly.

